From fbbdf91a3aacb4e859fd48bda569083377f5ee2f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work6_sched_tc_fixed <>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 19:27:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] apps/examples/testcase: add blocking and task lifecycle
 families

Phase 5 of the scenario test rollout.  Most real workloads are not CPU
bound; they block, and the scheduler's behaviour at the moment of a wake
matters more than its behaviour under saturation.  Task creation and
destruction are exercised constantly by the existing tests, but always on an
idle system, which is not where the list manipulation is delicate.

stc_sched_blocking adds four scenarios.  BLK-01 parks eight tasks on one
semaphore, two at each of four priorities, and posts from a task that
outranks all of them so that every post completes before any waiter runs;
the resulting order is therefore the scheduler's decision rather than an
artefact of interleaving, and it must be descending across priorities and
arrival ordered within one.  BLK-02 measures a wake crossing to an idle
remote CPU, where a missing inter processor signal does not lose the wake
but defers it until that CPU next reschedules for some unrelated reason.
BLK-03 runs a timed wait under full saturation, because a timeout that is
itself subject to starvation removes the last line of defence a driver has.
BLK-04 queues fifteen messages at three interleaved priorities before
starting the consumer, so delivery order is the queue's decision, and counts
them back to detect silent loss.

stc_sched_lifecycle adds four.  LIFE-01 creates and retires a thousand tasks
while every CPU is saturated and compares task control block count and heap
usage; a per iteration leak of even one control block is invisible in a
single create and destroy test and unmistakable after a thousand.  LIFE-02
deletes a task while it is executing on another CPU, which is the most
intricate removal path in the scheduler: the other CPU has to be interrupted
and made to pick something else before the control block can be released.
LIFE-03 deletes a blocked waiter and then posts once, because a stale entry
left on the wait list would send that post to a task that no longer exists
and leave the surviving waiter blocked forever; asserting only that the
delete returned success would miss it.  LIFE-04 cancels a thread under
saturation and asserts on the join completing rather than on the cancel
returning, since a cancel that is accepted but never acted upon leaves the
join blocked.

The oracles in the lifecycle family are about what is left behind rather
than about timing, because a defect there does not announce itself; it
leaks a control block or a wait list entry and the system degrades later for
no visible reason.  Both the SMP and non-SMP compilation paths are warning
free under -Wall -Wextra.
